-( Spacetime Assembly Syntax Examples )

-( Assembly Instruction Format )
-{
    General format: :: <opcode> << <operands> >> -> <destination>
    
    Examples:
    :: load.const << 42 >> -> %r0                    -(load constant into register)
    :: add << %r0, %r1 >> -> %r2                     -(add two registers)
    :: call << print >> << "Hello" >>                -(call function with argument)
    :: return << %r0 >>                              -(return value from function)
    :: load.global << $counter >> -> %r0             -(load global variable)
    :: store.global << %r0 >> -> $counter            -(store to global variable)
    :: cmp << %r0, 100 >>                            -(compare values)
    :: syscall << io_write >> << "text" >>           -(system call)
}

-( Register Usage Examples )
-{
    Virtual registers use %r prefix:
    
    %r0, %r1, %r2, ..., %r15    - General-purpose registers
    %rsp                         - Stack pointer (special)
    %rbp                         - Base pointer (special)
    
    Register conventions:
    %r0     - Return value, first argument
    %r1-%r3 - Additional arguments
    %r4-%r7 - Caller-saved temporary registers
    %r8-%r15 - Callee-saved registers
    
    Examples:
    :: load.const << 10 >> -> %r0
    :: load.const << 20 >> -> %r1
    :: add << %r0, %r1 >> -> %r2
    :: mul << %r2, 2 >> -> %r3
    :: return << %r3 >>
}

-( Label and Jump Examples )
-{
    Labels use @ prefix for control flow:
    
    @label_name                  - Define a label
    :: jump << @label_name >>    - Unconditional jump
    :: jz << @label >>           - Jump if zero
    :: jnz << @label >>          - Jump if not zero
    :: jlt << @label >>          - Jump if less than
    :: jle << @label >>          - Jump if less than or equal
    :: jgt << @label >>          - Jump if greater than
    :: jge << @label >>          - Jump if greater than or equal
    
    Example - Simple loop:
    @loop_start:
        :: load.const << 0 >> -> %r0
        :: add << %r0, 1 >> -> %r0
        :: cmp << %r0, 10 >>
        :: jlt << @loop_start >>
        :: return << %r0 >>
    
    Example - Conditional branching:
    @check_value:
        :: load.local << -8 >> -> %r0
        :: cmp << %r0, 100 >>
        :: jgt << @value_is_big >>
        
    @value_is_small:
        :: call << print >> << "Small" >>
        :: jump << @end >>
        
    @value_is_big:
        :: call << print >> << "Big" >>
        
    @end:
        :: return
}

-( Complete Function Example )
-{
    :: func add << a:int, b:int >> -> int {
        :: load.local << -8 >> -> %r0       -(load first parameter)
        :: load.local << -16 >> -> %r1      -(load second parameter)
        :: add << %r0, %r1 >> -> %r2        -(perform addition)
        :: return << %r2 >>                 -(return result)
    }
    
    :: func print << _possible_args_ >> -> void {
        :: load.const << 0 >> -> %r10       -(initialize counter)
        
    @print_loop:
        :: load.arg << %r10 >> -> %r0       -(load argument at index)
        :: test << %r0 >>                   -(check if null)
        :: jz << @print_end >>              -(exit if no more args)
        :: syscall << io_write >> << %r0 >> -(output to stdout)
        :: add << %r10, 1 >> -> %r10        -(increment counter)
        :: jump << @print_loop >>           -(continue loop)
        
    @print_end:
        :: return
    }
    
    @main:
        :: load.const << 10 >> -> %r0
        :: load.const << 20 >> -> %r1
        :: call << add >> << %r0, %r1 >>
        :: call << print >> << _possible_args_ >> {
            :: load.const << "Result: " >> -> %r0
            :: load.local << -8 >> -> %r1
            :: load.const << "\n" >> -> %r2
        }
        :: return
}

